1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,090
Motion graphs look like a zoo of curves.

2
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:08,308
But every one of them is read with
just two moves: take the gradient,

3
00:00:08,348 --> 00:00:09,352
or take the area.

4
00:00:09,899 --> 00:00:12,991
Watch a cart speed up, cruise, and brake.

5
00:00:13,173 --> 00:00:17,231
Its displacement, velocity and
acceleration graphs are drawn together,

6
00:00:17,271 --> 00:00:19,964
and they are really one
story told three ways.

7
00:00:20,146 --> 00:00:22,164
Gradient takes you down the ladder,

8
00:00:22,204 --> 00:00:24,909
from displacement to
velocity to acceleration.

9
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,627
Area takes you back up.

10
00:00:27,174 --> 00:00:32,060
Then we'll use the same two moves on
forces: momentum, force and impulse.

11
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:34,598
Same ladder, different quantities.

12
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:38,560
First, what do gradient
and area actually mean?

13
00:00:38,742 --> 00:00:40,360
The gradient is rise over run:

14
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,544
how much the vertical quantity changes
for each unit of the horizontal one.

15
00:00:44,726 --> 00:00:48,347
The area is the vertical quantity
times the horizontal quantity.

16
00:00:48,892 --> 00:00:51,830
Here's a trick that never
fails: read the units.

17
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:55,978
On a displacement-time graph, the
gradient is metres per second.

18
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:57,304
That's velocity.

19
00:00:57,486 --> 00:01:02,470
On a velocity-time graph, the area
is metres per second, times seconds.

20
00:01:02,652 --> 00:01:04,790
That's metres, so displacement.

21
00:01:05,335 --> 00:01:09,250
So whenever you meet a new
graph, ask: what are the axes?

22
00:01:09,432 --> 00:01:11,742
The gradient is one divided by the other.

23
00:01:11,924 --> 00:01:13,786
The area is one times the other.

24
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,927
Start with the simplest
motion: constant velocity.

25
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:21,076
On the displacement-time
graph that's a straight line,

26
00:01:21,116 --> 00:01:22,953
and its gradient is the velocity.

27
00:01:23,135 --> 00:01:25,880
A steeper line means a faster object.

28
00:01:26,425 --> 00:01:27,987
Flat means stationary.

29
00:01:28,169 --> 00:01:32,213
A line sloping down means moving
backwards: a negative velocity.

30
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:33,435
Underneath,

31
00:01:33,475 --> 00:01:37,994
the velocity-time graph is a horizontal
line at each of those values.

32
00:01:38,176 --> 00:01:41,726
The area under it, a rectangle,
is velocity times time,

33
00:01:41,766 --> 00:01:43,985
which is exactly the displacement.

34
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:47,081
And the acceleration-time
graph sits on zero,

35
00:01:47,121 --> 00:01:49,419
because the velocity never changes.

36
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:54,257
Now let the velocity change
steadily: constant acceleration.

37
00:01:54,439 --> 00:01:58,786
The acceleration-time graph is a
flat line, and the area under it,

38
00:01:58,826 --> 00:02:02,215
acceleration times time,
is the change in velocity.

39
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:06,701
That makes the velocity-time
graph a straight line, sloping up.

40
00:02:06,883 --> 00:02:09,090
Its gradient is the acceleration.

41
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:13,312
And on the displacement-time
graph, the gradient keeps growing,

42
00:02:13,352 --> 00:02:15,595
so the line curves up into a parabola.

43
00:02:15,776 --> 00:02:20,092
At every moment, the slope of the tangent
is the height of the velocity graph.

44
00:02:20,274 --> 00:02:23,474
A gentle slope early
on, a steep slope later.

45
00:02:24,019 --> 00:02:28,098
Check it with the units: metres
per second squared, times seconds,

46
00:02:28,138 --> 00:02:29,636
gives metres per second.

47
00:02:30,533 --> 00:02:32,748
The velocity-time graph hands you the

48
00:02:32,788 --> 00:02:35,469
equations of constant
acceleration for free.

49
00:02:35,651 --> 00:02:40,566
Start at the initial velocity, and finish
at the final velocity after a time t.

50
00:02:41,111 --> 00:02:43,098
The gradient is the acceleration,

51
00:02:43,138 --> 00:02:47,191
so the change in velocity divided
by the time is the acceleration.

52
00:02:47,373 --> 00:02:48,257
Rearrange it:

53
00:02:48,297 --> 00:02:53,126
final velocity equals initial
velocity plus acceleration times time.

54
00:02:53,670 --> 00:02:56,389
Now the displacement is
the area under the line.

55
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:59,215
Split it into a rectangle and a triangle.

56
00:02:59,397 --> 00:03:02,738
The rectangle is the initial
velocity times the time.

57
00:03:02,919 --> 00:03:06,784
The triangle is a half, times
the base, times the height,

58
00:03:06,824 --> 00:03:09,701
and the height is acceleration times time.

59
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:14,660
Add them: displacement equals initial
velocity times time, plus a half,

60
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:16,870
acceleration times time squared.

61
00:03:17,415 --> 00:03:21,706
Or treat the whole shape as one
trapezoid: average height times width.

62
00:03:21,888 --> 00:03:25,982
Displacement equals the average
of the initial and final velocity,

63
00:03:26,022 --> 00:03:26,961
times the time.

64
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:29,473
Finally, eliminate the time.

65
00:03:29,654 --> 00:03:32,796
Put the time from the first
equation into the trapezoid,

66
00:03:32,836 --> 00:03:35,637
and final velocity squared
equals initial velocity

67
00:03:35,677 --> 00:03:38,801
squared plus two times
acceleration times displacement.

68
00:03:38,983 --> 00:03:41,439
Four equations, all from one picture.

69
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:44,367
Back on the displacement-time graph,

70
00:03:44,407 --> 00:03:47,172
a curve has a different
gradient at every point.

71
00:03:47,353 --> 00:03:49,841
Pick two points on the
curve and join them.

72
00:03:50,023 --> 00:03:53,744
The gradient of that chord is the
average velocity between them.

73
00:03:54,289 --> 00:03:56,371
Now slide one point toward the other.

74
00:03:56,553 --> 00:04:00,309
The chord swings round, and in
the limit it becomes the tangent.

75
00:04:00,491 --> 00:04:03,949
The gradient of the tangent
is the instantaneous velocity.

76
00:04:04,488 --> 00:04:07,725
A common mistake: to find
an instantaneous value,

77
00:04:07,765 --> 00:04:12,044
use two points on the tangent
line, not two points on the curve.

78
00:04:12,226 --> 00:04:15,344
Two points on the curve only
ever give you an average.

79
00:04:16,233 --> 00:04:19,406
Throw a ball straight up,
and take upward as positive.

80
00:04:19,588 --> 00:04:22,994
The velocity starts positive,
shrinks to zero at the top,

81
00:04:23,034 --> 00:04:26,056
then becomes negative as
the ball comes back down.

82
00:04:26,238 --> 00:04:29,738
So the velocity-time graph is
a straight line sloping down.

83
00:04:29,919 --> 00:04:32,579
Its gradient is minus
nine point eight one:

84
00:04:32,619 --> 00:04:37,351
the acceleration is the same negative
number the whole way, even at the top,

85
00:04:37,391 --> 00:04:39,086
where the velocity is zero.

86
00:04:39,626 --> 00:04:41,657
Now the displacement-time graph.

87
00:04:41,839 --> 00:04:46,887
It rises, flattens at the top, where
the gradient is zero, and then falls.

88
00:04:47,425 --> 00:04:48,674
Look at the areas.

89
00:04:48,855 --> 00:04:51,784
The area above the axis
is positive displacement.

90
00:04:51,966 --> 00:04:53,715
The area below is negative.

91
00:04:53,897 --> 00:04:58,336
They cancel, so the ball ends where
it started: the displacement is zero.

92
00:04:58,518 --> 00:05:03,271
But the distance travelled adds up the
sizes of the areas, ignoring the signs.

93
00:05:03,452 --> 00:05:06,654
Up and down, that's thirty
nine point two metres.

94
00:05:07,567 --> 00:05:10,633
Real motion rarely has
constant acceleration.

95
00:05:10,815 --> 00:05:13,931
Drop a ball into oil,
or let a skydiver fall.

96
00:05:14,112 --> 00:05:17,813
At first the acceleration is
close to nine point eight one.

97
00:05:17,995 --> 00:05:22,439
As the speed grows, the drag
grows, so the acceleration shrinks.

98
00:05:22,981 --> 00:05:26,256
The velocity-time graph
curves over and levels off.

99
00:05:26,438 --> 00:05:31,861
Its gradient, the acceleration, falls
toward zero: that's terminal velocity.

100
00:05:32,043 --> 00:05:36,950
Zero acceleration does not mean zero
velocity: the object is still falling,

101
00:05:36,990 --> 00:05:38,178
at a steady speed.

102
00:05:38,716 --> 00:05:42,065
Displacement is still the area
under the velocity-time graph,

103
00:05:42,105 --> 00:05:45,106
and acceleration is still
the gradient of the tangent.

104
00:05:45,287 --> 00:05:48,975
But the constant acceleration
equations no longer apply.

105
00:05:49,157 --> 00:05:52,686
For a curve, estimate the
area by counting squares.

106
00:05:52,868 --> 00:05:55,945
And the displacement-time
graph ends up a straight line,

107
00:05:55,985 --> 00:05:58,268
because the velocity has become constant.

108
00:05:59,167 --> 00:06:01,805
A projectile is two motions at once.

109
00:06:01,987 --> 00:06:06,277
Horizontally there is no force, so
the horizontal velocity is constant:

110
00:06:06,317 --> 00:06:09,733
a flat velocity graph and a
straight displacement graph.

111
00:06:09,915 --> 00:06:13,504
Vertically there is only gravity,
so it's the thrown ball again:

112
00:06:13,544 --> 00:06:16,510
a velocity line sloping down
through zero at the top,

113
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:18,252
and a parabola for the height.

114
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:23,882
At the top, the vertical velocity is
zero, but the horizontal velocity is not,

115
00:06:23,922 --> 00:06:25,762
so the ball has not stopped.

116
00:06:25,943 --> 00:06:28,177
And the two sets of graphs never mix.

117
00:06:28,358 --> 00:06:30,976
Time is the only thing
the two motions share.

118
00:06:31,867 --> 00:06:33,591
Now forces and momentum.

119
00:06:33,773 --> 00:06:35,900
Momentum is mass times velocity,

120
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:41,156
so a momentum-time graph is just the
velocity-time graph, scaled by the mass.

121
00:06:41,694 --> 00:06:44,102
Newton's second law, in its general form,

122
00:06:44,142 --> 00:06:47,666
says the resultant force is
the rate of change of momentum.

123
00:06:47,847 --> 00:06:48,825
That's a gradient.

124
00:06:49,006 --> 00:06:52,802
So the gradient of a momentum-time
graph is the resultant force.

125
00:06:53,344 --> 00:06:57,587
Check the units: kilogram metres
per second, divided by seconds,

126
00:06:57,627 --> 00:07:01,443
is kilogram metres per second
squared, which is a newton.

127
00:07:01,625 --> 00:07:03,865
A steep line means a big force.

128
00:07:04,047 --> 00:07:06,718
A flat line means no resultant force.

129
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:10,073
A line sloping down means the
force points the other way.

130
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,266
Now flip it round.

131
00:07:12,447 --> 00:07:14,469
If force is the gradient of momentum,

132
00:07:14,509 --> 00:07:18,411
then the area under a force-time
graph must be the change in momentum.

133
00:07:18,593 --> 00:07:20,688
That area is called the impulse.

134
00:07:21,227 --> 00:07:22,409
Check the units.

135
00:07:22,590 --> 00:07:23,944
Newtons times seconds,

136
00:07:23,984 --> 00:07:28,293
and a newton second is the same
unit as a kilogram metre per second.

137
00:07:28,475 --> 00:07:32,703
For a constant force the area is
a rectangle: force times time.

138
00:07:32,885 --> 00:07:37,334
For a varying force, split the
area into shapes, or count squares.

139
00:07:37,871 --> 00:07:42,678
Take a triangular pulse, six thousand
newtons high and ten milliseconds wide.

140
00:07:42,859 --> 00:07:47,135
The area is a half, times six
thousand, times point zero one:

141
00:07:47,175 --> 00:07:48,732
thirty newton seconds.

142
00:07:48,914 --> 00:07:51,159
So the momentum changes by thirty.

143
00:07:51,702 --> 00:07:54,250
Area below the axis counts as negative.

144
00:07:54,431 --> 00:07:57,341
A ball of mass point one
five kilograms hits a

145
00:07:57,381 --> 00:08:00,780
wall at twenty metres per
second and rebounds at ten.

146
00:08:00,962 --> 00:08:03,196
Take the first direction as positive.

147
00:08:03,378 --> 00:08:07,007
The momentum goes from plus
three to minus one point five,

148
00:08:07,047 --> 00:08:10,526
so the impulse is minus four
point five newton seconds.

149
00:08:10,708 --> 00:08:14,312
If the contact lasts ten
milliseconds, the average force,

150
00:08:14,352 --> 00:08:19,020
the impulse divided by the time, is
minus four hundred and fifty newtons.

151
00:08:19,933 --> 00:08:21,369
Now chain them together.

152
00:08:21,551 --> 00:08:25,697
Start with a force-time graph: a
push that builds up, holds steady,

153
00:08:25,737 --> 00:08:26,674
and fades away.

154
00:08:26,856 --> 00:08:30,357
The momentum-time graph is the
running total of that area.

155
00:08:30,539 --> 00:08:34,664
While the force is growing, the
momentum climbs faster and faster.

156
00:08:34,846 --> 00:08:38,089
While the force is steady,
it climbs in a straight line.

157
00:08:38,271 --> 00:08:41,708
When the force fades to zero,
the momentum levels off.

158
00:08:42,248 --> 00:08:46,132
Divide by the mass, and the same
shape is the velocity-time graph.

159
00:08:46,314 --> 00:08:47,701
And notice the pattern.

160
00:08:47,883 --> 00:08:51,052
Force is to momentum what
velocity is to displacement.

161
00:08:51,234 --> 00:08:54,470
The gradient goes one way,
and the area goes the other.

162
00:08:55,367 --> 00:08:58,423
The same impulse can come
from very different graphs.

163
00:08:58,605 --> 00:09:03,438
Drop an egg on concrete, and it stops
in a thousandth of a second: a tall,

164
00:09:03,478 --> 00:09:04,335
narrow spike.

165
00:09:04,517 --> 00:09:09,470
Drop it on a pillow, and it stops over
a tenth of a second: a low, wide hill.

166
00:09:09,652 --> 00:09:13,350
The areas are equal, because the
change in momentum is the same,

167
00:09:13,390 --> 00:09:15,844
so the pillow's peak force is far smaller.

168
00:09:16,026 --> 00:09:20,243
That's the idea behind airbags,
crumple zones and bending your knees.

169
00:09:20,784 --> 00:09:22,882
And a bounce hits harder than a stop.

170
00:09:23,064 --> 00:09:26,795
Stopping removes the momentum:
call that one unit of area.

171
00:09:26,976 --> 00:09:31,029
Bouncing back at the same speed
reverses it, so the change is double,

172
00:09:31,069 --> 00:09:32,197
and so is the area.

173
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:35,766
Finally, a collision between two carts.

174
00:09:35,948 --> 00:09:38,647
During the contact, cart
one pushes cart two,

175
00:09:38,687 --> 00:09:43,312
and cart two pushes back on cart one
with the same force, for the same time.

176
00:09:43,494 --> 00:09:45,021
That's Newton's third law.

177
00:09:45,203 --> 00:09:50,161
So the two force-time graphs are mirror
images: equal height, opposite sign.

178
00:09:50,707 --> 00:09:53,372
Their areas are therefore
equal and opposite,

179
00:09:53,412 --> 00:09:55,757
so the impulses are equal and opposite.

180
00:09:55,939 --> 00:09:59,390
One cart gains exactly the
momentum the other loses.

181
00:09:59,930 --> 00:10:03,082
Now plot the momentum of
each cart, and the total.

182
00:10:03,264 --> 00:10:07,152
One line rises, the other
falls, and the sum stays flat.

183
00:10:07,334 --> 00:10:10,528
That's conservation of
momentum, seen on a graph.

184
00:10:11,433 --> 00:10:13,240
So here are the two ladders.

185
00:10:13,422 --> 00:10:19,213
Displacement, velocity, acceleration:
gradients go down, and areas go up.

186
00:10:19,395 --> 00:10:22,648
Momentum and force: the
gradient of momentum is force,

187
00:10:22,688 --> 00:10:25,798
and the area under force
is the change in momentum.

188
00:10:26,341 --> 00:10:29,524
Whenever a graph appears,
ask three questions.

189
00:10:29,706 --> 00:10:30,998
What are the axes?

190
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:34,751
So what do the gradient and
the area mean, by their units?

191
00:10:34,933 --> 00:10:38,348
And does the graph go below
zero, so that signs matter?

192
00:10:38,530 --> 00:10:41,753
Answer those, and you can
read any graph in the course.
